================================================================================
                       Bluetooth for AmigaOS 4
                    BTstack port, libusb-1.library
================================================================================

WHAT THIS IS

  A Bluetooth stack for AmigaOS 4, built on BTstack and talking to the
  controller through libusb-1.library. It lets a Bluetooth mouse and keyboard
  drive the machine as if they were plugged in: the pointer moves for the whole
  system and keys reach every application, because the events go into
  input.device rather than into any one program.

  Three parts, each doing one job.

    BluetoothService   the stack itself, running as a background process
    BluetoothGUI       a window for managing devices
    bt.usbfd           a USB function driver that starts the service when a
                       controller is plugged in

  Plus bthid, a smaller program that drives a single LE device without the
  service, kept because it is useful for testing.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  AmigaOS 4.1
  A USB Bluetooth controller. Realtek parts are the best tested; the firmware
    for many of them ships with this archive.
  libusb-1.library

  Firmware matters more than it sounds. A Realtek controller holds none of its
  own and is given it at every startup - without it the controller comes up,
  answers commands, and finds nothing at all. It scans for a minute and reports
  not one device, which looks like broken hardware and is not.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GETTING STARTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Plug the controller in. If bt.usbfd is installed the service starts by
  itself; otherwise run BluetoothService from a Shell.

  Start BluetoothGUI, give the machine a name, and press Scan. Put the device
  into pairing mode - most have a button for it, held for a few seconds - and
  when it appears in the top list, select it and press Pair.

  Once paired it reconnects on its own from then on, and there is nothing to do
  at each startup.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BluetoothService
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The stack. It owns the controller, keeps the list of known devices, and feeds
  what they send into input.device.

    BluetoothService [-v] [-l FILE] [-f FOLDER] [-p] [-d VID:PID]
                     [--secure-only] [--forget-all]

    -v              log every advertisement and every decoded report
    -l FILE         write a packet log, readable in Wireshark
    -f FOLDER       where to look for controller firmware
    -p              accept LE Legacy Pairing (many mice need this)
    -d VID:PID      use this USB device rather than searching
    --secure-only   require LE Secure Connections, refusing legacy pairing
    --forget-all    start with no bonds at all, so everything pairs afresh

  It publishes a message port called "bluetooth.service". BluetoothGUI is a
  client of that port and nothing more; anything else could be too.

  CTRL-C stops it: the first powers Bluetooth down and shuts it cleanly, a
  second forces it. Held keys and mouse buttons are always released on the way
  out, so nothing can be left stuck for the whole system.

  Where it keeps things:

    ENVARC:Bluetooth/btstack_<address>.tlv    bonds and known devices
    SYS:Firmware/bt/                          controller firmware

  When to use --forget-all. A device held in pairing mode is waiting to create
  a new bond and will not settle for being authenticated with the key from an
  old one: it accepts the connection, never finishes its own pairing, and drops
  the link when its pairing window closes. If a device pairs and then
  disconnects a minute later, a stale key is the likely reason and this clears
  it.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BluetoothGUI
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Two lists, answering two different questions.

  Nearby is what is being heard from right now. It fills as devices are seen
  and changes constantly - this is where a device appears when you put it in
  pairing mode. Select one and press Pair.

  Known devices is what has been paired. It persists, and it is what there is
  to manage: Connect, Disconnect, Forget.

  Scan starts a search for devices that are not known yet and runs for thirty
  seconds. It is a switch: while it runs the button says Stop scan.

  The service itself is started and stopped here, and the button says which of
  the two it will do. Whether it is running is checked every couple of seconds,
  so a service started by bt.usbfd when a controller was plugged in shows up
  without anything being pressed.

  The name field is what other devices see, and what they offer to connect to.
  It is remembered.

  Buttons that act on a selection are disabled while there is none.

  The window is localised. Translations go in
  LOCALE:Catalogs/<language>/BluetoothGUI.catalog, and the English built into
  the program is used for anything a catalog does not cover.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
bt.usbfd
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A USB function driver, installed in DEVS:USB/fd. When a Bluetooth controller
  appears it starts BluetoothService, so a dongle left plugged in means
  Bluetooth is up by the time the Workbench is.

  It does nothing until the USB stack has finished booting and the service is
  actually reachable on disk. Both matter: a dongle plugged in at power on gets
  the driver called extremely early, when the boot volume may not be mounted,
  and touching DOS at that point does not fail politely - it stops the boot.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEVICES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Mice and keyboards, over both Bluetooth LE and Bluetooth Classic. These are
  genuinely different: an LE device is found by scanning for its
  advertisements, a Classic one only by inquiry, and a Classic keyboard never
  advertises at all.

  Reports are decoded through the device's own HID report descriptor, looking
  fields up by what they mean rather than by where they sit, so the layout of a
  particular device does not matter.

  Keyboards are mapped positionally: HID usage 0x14 is "the key where Q is on a
  US keyboard", and that is mapped to the Amiga raw key code for the same
  physical key. What it produces is then decided by the keymap you have chosen,
  so an Italian or German layout comes out right without anything here knowing
  about it. Dead keys work: every key event carries the two previously pressed
  keys, which is how keymap.library composes accented characters.

  Held keys repeat, at the delay and rate set in Prefs/Input. That is the
  keyboard driver's job on AmigaOS, not input.device's, so it is done here.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHEN SOMETHING DOES NOT WORK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Nothing is found, and the log shows no devices at all
    Almost always missing firmware. The paths BTstack tried are printed at
    startup - check the files are actually there, uncompressed, and named
    exactly as printed.

  A Classic keyboard pairs and works, then stops after a minute
    A stale link key. Run with --forget-all and pair it again.

  A device is listed but will not connect
    If it is Classic and asleep it is not listening; press a key on it. A
    bonded keyboard reconnects by calling the computer, not the other way
    round.

  Wrong characters, or a modifier stuck on
    If every report carries the same modifier, that is the keyboard's own
    state, not the decoding - press and release that modifier to clear it.
    Running with -v prints each report and the descriptor it is decoded with.

  The GUI says the service is a different version
    The three programs share a protocol and are updated together. Copy all of
    BluetoothService, BluetoothGUI and bt.usbfd, and restart the service.

  For anything else, -l FILE writes a packet log that Wireshark reads, which
  shows what actually went over the air rather than what was meant to.


